 "forward": {
  "title": "Starting today",
  "lead": "Smith County owes $346.3 million in principal and interest on the bonds above, through 2048 — that gets paid no matter what. The live question is the next road program. From 2018 to 2025 the county borrowed about $10 million a year for roads.",
  "points": [
   "Keep borrowing: a $10 million, 20-year bond at 4.5% costs about $0.77 million the first year. Borrow again every year and the payments stack up to about $15.5 million a year by 2046 — and stay there.",
   "Pay cash instead: $10 million a year from the first year — about 3 cents per $100 of property value, on top of the old bond payments until they end in 2048.",
   "Borrowing is cheaper to taxpayers until about 2054. From then on, paying cash saves about $5.4 million every year.",
   "For comparison: Tyler’s city government states it owes $0 in debt paid by property taxes."
  ],
